## Why This Is Asked

This problem evaluates dynamic programming expertise across string partitioning with fixed segment counts, numeric parsing constraints, and solution reconstruction—fundamental skills for real-world interview scenarios.

## Key Concepts

- Dynamic programming over string prefixes and partition counts
- Handling substring numeric values with leading-zero validation
- Solution path reconstruction for determining optimal cut positions
- Time and space complexity optimization techniques

## Code Example

```javascript
function minSumPartition(s, k) {
  const n = s.length;

  // Helper to get numeric value of substring [l, r)
  const getValue = (l, r) => {
    if (s[l] === '0' && r - l > 1) return null; // leading zero
    return Number(s.slice(l, r));
  };

  const dp = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(Infinity));
  const parent = Array(n + 1).fill().map(() => Array(k + 1).fill(-1));

  dp[0][0] = 0;

  for (let i = 1; i <= n; i++) {
    for (let t = 1; t <= k; t++) {
      for (let j = t - 1; j < i; j++) {
        const val = getValue(j, i);
        if (val !== null && dp[j][t-1] + val < dp[i][t]) {
          dp[i][t] = dp[j][t-1] + val;
          parent[i][t] = j;
        }
      }
    }
  }

  // Reconstruct cuts
  const cuts = [];
  let curr = n, currT = k;
  while (currT > 0) {
    const prev = parent[curr][currT];
    cuts.push(prev);
    curr = prev;
    currT--;
  }
  cuts.pop(); // remove 0
  cuts.reverse();

  return { minSum: dp[n][k], cuts };
}
```

## Notes

- The DP explores all possible cut positions while respecting the k-substring constraint
- Leading zero validation is crucial for correctness
- The parent array enables O(k) reconstruction of optimal cuts

## Why This Is Asked
This introduces selecting non-covering, fixed-cost segments, a natural DP with a running max optimization. It tests understanding of prefix sums, state pruning, and reconstruction.

## Key Concepts
- Prefix sums to compute segment sums in O(1)
- DP over i and t with exactly t segments
- Running maximum best[t] to avoid O(n) inner loop
- Path reconstruction to recover endpoints

## Code Example
```javascript
function maxValueSegments(A, k, c){
  const n = A.length;
  const S = Array(n+1).fill(0);
  for(let i=1;i<=n;i++) S[i]=S[i-1]+A[i-1];
  const negInf = -1e18;
  const dp = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill(negInf));
  const par = Array.from({length:n+1},()=>Array(k+1).fill([-1,-1]));
  dp[0][0]=0;
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++) par[0][t]=[-1,-1];
  for(let t=1;t<=k;t++){
    let best = negInf; // max of dp[m][t-1]-S[m]
    for(let i=1;i<=n;i++){
      best = Math.max(best, dp[i-1][t-1]-S[i-1]);
      const val = best + S[i] - c;
      if(val > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=val; par[i][t]=[i-1,-1]; }
      if(dp[i-1][t] > dp[i][t]){ dp[i][t]=dp[i-1][t]; par[i][t]=[i-1,t]; }
    }
  }
  // reconstruction (simple version)
  let i=n, t=k, ends=[];
  while(t>0){
    const [pi, pt] = par[i][t] || [-1,-1];
    if(pi===-1) break;
    // approximate end at i, start at some m where dp[m][t-1]-S[m] was max
    // for brevity here, return only values; full reconstruction can be added as needed
    ends.push(i);
    i = pi; t--;
  }
  return { value: dp[n][k], ends: ends.reverse() };
}
```

